# TLS 1.3 and post-quantum readiness (VCP)

VCP is an **Internet-facing CRUD portal** (HTTPS + HTML forms / pages).
There is **no WebSocket** product surface. Transport and crypto must
still match the bastion’s high bar: TLS 1.3 and post-quantum hybrids.

Implementation detail and code sketches live in the
`rust-best-practices` skill (§ cryptography / network hardening).
This rule is the normative gate.

## Transport (public edge)

- **HTTPS only** on the public listener. Do not ship a plain-HTTP
  production acceptor (local redirect / ACME challenges are the only
  exceptions, and must not serve app traffic).
- **TLS 1.3 minimum.** Disable TLS 1.2 and below on the public edge.
- Prefer **rustls** (and aws-lc / ring backends as appropriate) over
  OpenSSL for in-process TLS. Mirror that preference in `cargo deny`
  (`openssl` banned unless an explicit, documented exception).
- Always validate certificates on outbound TLS (webhooks, IdP, payment
  providers): no `accept_invalid_certs` in production paths.
- Set connect / read / write / idle timeouts on every network client.
- When TLS terminates at a reverse proxy instead of in-process: the
  proxy MUST enforce the same TLS 1.3-only policy; document the split
  in deploy docs and still keep rustls-ready config in the app for
  direct / internal HTTPS if used.

## Post-quantum posture

| Plane | Target |
|-------|--------|
| TLS key exchange | Hybrid classical + PQ (e.g. X25519 + ML-KEM-768) as soon as the chosen rustls / provider stack supports it in a release we pin |
| App-level KEM (if any) | Hybrid X25519 + ML-KEM-768; never RSA/ECDH alone for new designs |
| App-level signatures (if any) | Hybrid Ed25519 + ML-DSA-65 (or SLH-DSA where hash-based is required) |
| Symmetric | AES-256-GCM (min 256-bit keys) |
| Password hashing | Argon2id |
| Hashing (new designs) | SHA-3 or BLAKE3; do not introduce SHA-1 / MD5 |

Rules:

- **Never roll custom crypto.** Use audited crates / provider APIs.
- Prefer **hybrid** constructions during the migration window so
  classical breaks or PQ breaks alone do not sink confidentiality.
- Abstract TLS / KEM configuration behind a small module so enabling
  hybrid groups is a config + dependency bump, not a rewrite.
- Track rustls / aws-lc hybrid group availability; pin versions once
  enabled and cover with a smoke check (cipher / group negotiation).
- Zeroize secret material; constant-time compare for secrets; wrap
  secrets with `secrecy` / equivalent (see rust skill).

## Forbidden

| Forbidden | Required instead |
|-----------|------------------|
| TLS 1.0 / 1.1 / 1.2 on the public portal edge | TLS 1.3 only |
| OpenSSL as the default TLS stack | rustls (deny OpenSSL by default) |
| New RSA / ECDSA-only key exchange or signatures for app crypto | Hybrid PQ (+ classical) |
| `accept_invalid_certs` in production clients | Full verification |
| WebSocket / long-lived duplex as a product feature | Request/response CRUD (pages, forms, procedures/shards as needed) |
| Inventing a second crypto story “just for the portal” | Same algorithm table as this rule / rust skill |

## Checklist (scaffold and deploy)

- [x] Public HTTPS listener; no app traffic on cleartext (`src/tls/`)
- [ ] TLS 1.3-only config verified (sslscan / testssl / equivalent)
- [x] rustls (or proxy-enforced equivalent) documented
- [ ] `cargo deny` (or CI) prefers rustls / bans OpenSSL by default
- [ ] Outbound TLS clients verify certificates
- [ ] TLS config module ready to flip on hybrid PQ groups
- [ ] Any app-level KEM/sign uses hybrid NIST PQ algorithms
- [x] HSTS on production responses (`security_headers` layer; no `preload` yet)
- [x] Secrets redacted in logs; no private keys in the repo (`certs/` gitignored)

## Out of scope (bastion-only)

Capsicum, mTLS IPC to proxies, session recording channels, and
bastion WebSocket viewers stay in `../Vauban`. Do not import them to
satisfy this rule — TLS 1.3 + PQ applies to the **portal HTTPS edge**
and application crypto, not to bastion privsep.
